General elections were held in [[Sierra Leone]] on 11 August 2007. Seven candidates competed in the first round of the presidential election;<ref name=Ready>[http://english.people.com.cn/90001/90777/6236100.html "Sierra Leone ready for elections: electoral official"], Xinhua (''People's Daily Online''), August 10, 2007.</ref> no candidate received the necessary 55% of the vote to win in the first round,<ref name=Set>[http://www.voanews.com/english/2007-08-25-voa15.cfm "Sierra Leone Presidential Runoff Set for September 8"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070827032812/http://www.voanews.com/english/2007-08-25-voa15.cfm |date=2007-08-27 }}, VOA News, August 25, 2007.</ref> and a second round was held between the top two candidates, [[Ernest Bai Koroma]] of the [[All People's Congress]] (APC) and [[Solomon Berewa]] of the [[Sierra Leone People's Party]] (SLPP), on 8 September. According to official results, Koroma won the election with 54.6% of the vote.<ref name=Rod>Rod MacJohnson, [http://www.sundaytimes.co.za/News/Article.aspx?id=566281 "Sierra Leone gets a new leader"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180706075339/http://www.sundaytimes.co.za/News/Article.aspx?id=566281 |date=2018-07-06 }}, AFP (''The Times'', South Africa), September 17, 2007.</ref><ref name=Win>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/6998687.stm "S Leone opposition win presidency"], BBC News, 17 September 2007.</ref>

566 candidates stood in the parliamentary election, in which 112 seats, out of a total of 124, were at stake.<ref name=Ready/> Voting for seats in parliament was done on a [[first-past-the-post]] constituency basis, rather than the system of [[proportional representation]] used previously.<ref name=Split>[http://www.news24.com/News24/Africa/News/0,,2-11-1447_2171641,00.html "Polls split Sierra Leone"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070829170803/http://www.news24.com/News24/Africa/News/0,,2-11-1447_2171641,00.html |date=2007-08-29 }}, News24.com, August 27, 2007.</ref> 12 members of parliament were chosen by traditional chiefs, who have been considered to be allied with the SLPP, in a separate election.<ref>[http://afp.google.com/article/ALeqM5i6nSBM90iHD5O_h9-bxWHEhdOwKw "Opposition sweeps Sierra Leone election"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110520103537/http://afp.google.com/article/ALeqM5i6nSBM90iHD5O_h9-bxWHEhdOwKw |date=2011-05-20 }}, AFP, August 23, 2007.</ref> The election was described as "free and fair" and "orderly" by international observers.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.khaleejtimes.com/DisplayArticleNew.asp?xfile=data%2Ftheworld%2F2007%2FAugust%2Ftheworld_August231.xml&section=theworld&col= |title=Khaleej Times Online - Counting underway after Sierra Leone vote |website=www.khaleejtimes.com |access-date=5 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070930224031/http://www.khaleejtimes.com/DisplayArticleNew.asp?xfile=data%2Ftheworld%2F2007%2FAugust%2Ftheworld_August231.xml&section=theworld&col= |archive-date=30 September 2007 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://en.people.cn/90001/90777/6237495.html|title = People's Daily Online - Sierra Leone holds "successful" landmark general elections}}</ref>

==Background==
Vice-President Solomon Berewa was chosen as the presidential candidate of the ruling SLPP, as well as the party's leader, in early September 2005.<ref>[http://www.int.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=68&art_id=qw1126098901747B245 "Sierra Leone leader sacks three key ministers"], Reuters (''IOL''), September 7, 2005.</ref> The incumbent president, [[Ahmed Tejan Kabbah]] of the SLPP, was prohibited from running again by term limits.<ref name=Awaits>[http://www.angolapress-angop.ao/noticia-e.asp?ID=552531 "Sierra Leone awaits poll results"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926222916/http://www.angolapress-angop.ao/noticia-e.asp?ID=552531 |date=2007-09-26 }}, AngolaPress, August 14, 2007.</ref> On July 2, 2007, Foreign Minister [[Momodu Koroma]] was named as the party's vice-presidential candidate.<ref>[http://apanews.net/apa.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=36515 "Sierra Leone: ruling party names running mate amid controversy"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, July 2, 2007.</ref> Ernest Bai Koroma was nominated as the presidential candidate of the main opposition party APC,<ref name=Nom>[http://apanews.net/apa.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=36743 "Sierra Leone: leading parties nominate candidates for presidential poll"] {{webarchive|url=https://archive.today/20070805235821/http://apanews.net/apa.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=36743 |date=2007-08-05 }}, African Press Agency, July 5, 2007.</ref> with [[Samuel Sam-Sumana]] as his vice-presidential candidate.<ref>[http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20055930.shtml "Biographical Sketch of Mr. Samuel Sam-Sumana: Running Mate of the All People’s Congress (APC) party in the August 11 Presidential Election"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070813144344/http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20055930.shtml |date=2007-08-13 }}, APC (''Awareness Times''), July 5, 2007.</ref> [[Charles Margai]] was nominated as the presidential candidate of the [[People's Movement for Democratic Change]] (PMDC),<ref name=Nom/> with [[Ibrahim Tejan-Jalloh]] as his vice-presidential candidate.<ref>[http://www.thepatrioticvanguard.com/article.php3?id_article=1153 "PMDC-USA Welcomes Dr. Ibrahim Tejan-Jalloh"], press release (''The Patriotic Vanguard''), April 12, 2007.</ref> Margai formed the PMDC as a split from the SLPP in 2005, and this was seen as weakening the latter party.<ref>Abdul Lamin, [http://www.mg.co.za/articlePage.aspx?articleid=316760&area=/insight/insight__africa/ "Vote of confidence"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070821063418/http://www.mg.co.za/articlePage.aspx?articleid=316760&area=%2Finsight%2Finsight__africa%2F |date=2007-08-21 }}, ''Mail & Guardian Online'' (South Africa), August 16, 2007.</ref> Prior to the election, Berewa, Koroma, and Margai were considered the main three presidential candidates.<ref>[http://apanews.net/apa.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=39197 "Sierra Leone: 3 of seven candidates are presidential frontrunners"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, August 11, 2007.</ref>

In May 2007, the National Electoral Commission (NEC) delayed the election by two weeks from the previously set date of 28 July, saying that it was necessary to have additional time after parliament was dissolved in late June 2007. This decision was sharply criticized by the opposition, since it meant the election would be held when the rainy season was at its peak, potentially making voting difficult for many people.<ref>[http://www.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=86&art_id=nw20070508220407443C868539 "S Leone opposition slams poll delays"], AFP (''IOL''), 9 May 2007.</ref> On 11 May, NEC Chairperson Christiana Thorpe rejected requests for the election to be delayed until December, saying that it would be unconstitutional to hold them so late. Acknowledging the impact the rain would likely have on voter turnout, Thorpe said that there would be more polling stations in order to make voting more convenient.<ref>[http://www.news24.com/News24/Africa/News/0,,2-11-1447_2112302,00.html "Vote date set in Sierra Leone"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080208210549/http://www.news24.com/News24/Africa/News/0,,2-11-1447_2112302,00.html |date=2008-02-08 }}, Sapa (News24.com), 12 May 2007.</ref>

===Campaigning===
Campaigning for the election began on 10 July.<ref>[http://www.int.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=68&art_id=nw20070712220601686C645477 "Pre-poll violence on the rise in Sierra Leone"], AFP (''IOL''), July 12, 2007.</ref> The campaign was marred by some violence, but on 2 August an army spokesman described the country as "relatively calm and peaceful" and predicted a "peaceful, credible and violence-free" election. He also said that the army would not intervene in politics.<ref>[http://www.int.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=68&art_id=nw20070802215336454C796988 "Sierra Leone declared secure for polls"], AFP (''IOL''), August 2, 2007.</ref>

APC candidate Koroma was allegedly the target of an assassination attempt in the early hours of July 23, 2007, when, according to the APC, a group of armed men led by [[Tom Nyuma]], who was a participant in the 1992 coup that ousted the APC, attempted to enter his hotel room in the southern city of [[Bo, Sierra Leone|Bo]] to kill him.<ref>Alpha Kanu, [http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20056099.shtml "Statement issued by the All People's Congress in Sierra Leone"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070928043452/http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20056099.shtml |date=2007-09-28 }}, ''Awareness Times'' (news.sl), July 24, 2007.</ref> In a letter to the Inspector General of Police in early August, Berewa alleged that the APC and the PMDC were planning to disrupt the election and intimidate SLPP supporters. He claimed that they planned to send their own supporters dressed in Operational Support Division uniforms to polling stations.<ref>[http://apanews.net/apa.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=39156 "Sierra Leone’s vice president accuses opposition leaders of planning chaos"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, August 10, 2007.</ref> APC Secretary-General [[Victor Foh]] accused the SLPP of planning electoral fraud.<ref name=Underway>[http://www.khaleejtimes.com/DisplayArticleNew.asp?xfile=data/theworld/2007/August/theworld_August231.xml&section=theworld&col= "Counting underway after Sierra Leone vote"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070930224031/http://www.khaleejtimes.com/DisplayArticleNew.asp?xfile=data%2Ftheworld%2F2007%2FAugust%2Ftheworld_August231.xml&section=theworld&col= |date=2007-09-30 }}, AFP (''Khaleej Times Online''), August 12, 2007.</ref>

==Election violence==
Acts of violence escalated in the run up to the polls, especially in the capital, [[Freetown]], and the south-eastern cities of [[Bo, Sierra Leone|Bo]]  [[Kenema]] and [[Koidu|Koidu Town]]. In July, [[police]] arrested scores of people after clashes in Bo between SLPP and PMDC supporters.

===Election day and counts===
There were about 2.6 million registered voters at the time of the election and 6,171 [[polling station]]s.<ref name=Ready/><ref name=Underway/> Voting was reported to be peaceful,<ref name=Underway/><ref name=Success>[http://english.people.com.cn/90001/90777/6237495.html "Sierra Leone holds "successful" landmark general elections"], Xinhua (''People's Daily Online''), August 12, 2007.</ref> although in some places young people were said to have tried to disrupt vote counting, leading to increased security measures.<ref name=Underway/> An electoral commission official described the voting process as "smooth and successful". Turnout was reported to be high, despite the rains,<ref name=Success/> with long lines at polling stations.<ref name=Underway/> Observers gave the election a positive appraisal<ref name=Free>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/6943541.stm "S Leone elections 'free and fair'"], BBC News, August 13, 2007.</ref><ref name=IHT>[http://www.iht.com/articles/2007/08/13/news/leone.php "Early results show lead for opposition in Sierra Leone"], Reuters and Associated Press (''[[International Herald Tribune]]''), August 13, 2007.</ref> and placed voter turnout at more than 70%. Marie-Anne Isler, head of the [[European Union]] observer mission, described voting as "well organized, positive and transparent", but also said that the SLPP enjoyed a strong advantage due to incumbency and the support of some traditional chiefs, and that it was "extremely dominant" in state television coverage.<ref name=IHT/> EU observers also said that Berewa appeared to have distributed money to voters at his home, a claim that the SLPP denounced as "baseless and unfounded".<ref name=Dispatch>[http://www.dispatch.co.za/2007/08/14/Foreign/apoll.html "Sierra Leone poll ‘falls short’"], AFP (''Dispatch'', South Africa), August 14, 2007.</ref>

Vote counting was done publicly at polling stations, with party representatives present.<ref name=Free/> On August 13, Kabbah discouraged "provocative or inflammatory statements" and said that police would "deal firmly with any threats to the peace and stability of the nation".<ref name=Awaits/> On the same day, NEC Chairperson Thorpe announced results from 7.4% of polling stations; out of these 150,374 votes, Koroma had 107,341 and Berewa had 33,041.<ref>[http://www.afriquenligne.fr/news/daily_news/opposition_presidential_candidate_leads_in_sierra_leone_polls_200708145860/ "Opposition presidential candidate leads in Sierra Leone polls"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, Panapress (Afriquenligne.fr), August 14, 2007 {{in lang|fr}}.</ref> Further results on August 14, from about 19% of polling stations, showed Koroma remaining well ahead of Berewa, with 204,774 votes against 106,487 for Berewa. Margai, in third place, had 43,904 votes.<ref>[http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/08/14/africa/AF-POL-Sierra-Leone-Election.php "Opposition candidate leads in Sierra Leone presidential race"], Associated Press (''International Herald Tribune''), August 14, 2007.</ref> Meanwhile, [[United Nations]] Secretary-General [[Ban Ki-moon]] appealed for the preservation of "an atmosphere of calm and public order" and said that "any potential dispute" should be resolved "within the established legal channels".<ref>[http://apanews.net/apa.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=39522 "Sierra Leone elections: UN Secretary-General appeals for calm"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, August 16, 2007.</ref><ref>[http://www.afrol.com/articles/26431 "Sierra Leone leader urges calm"], afrol News, August 14, 2007.</ref>

On August 15, an APC spokesman claimed that, based on its own count, the party had won 61 seats in parliament, which would be a majority, although at the time official results for only four seats had been announced, two for the APC, one for the SLPP, and one for the PMDC.<ref>James Butty, [http://www.voanews.com/english/Africa/2007-08-16-voa5.cfm "Sierra Leone's Election Leader Asks Supporters for Patience"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081010220226/http://www.voanews.com/english/Africa/2007-08-16-voa5.cfm |date=2008-10-10 }}, VOA News, August 16, 2007.</ref> Partial results for the presidential election released by the National Electoral Commission on August 15, accounting for 34.3% of polling stations, showed Koroma still leading, although more narrowly; Koroma had 297,206 votes, compared to 227,353 for Berewa and 97,669 for Margai.<ref>[http://www.news.sl/drwebsite/uploads/aug15_results.pdf Results from the National Electoral Commission on August 15, 2007] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926041353/http://www.news.sl/drwebsite/uploads/aug15_results.pdf |date=September 26, 2007 }}, news.sl.</ref> Further results on August 16, accounting for 45.3% of polling stations, showed Koroma with 400,027 votes, Berewa with 310,321 votes, and Margai with 120,231 votes.<ref>[http://www.news.sl/drwebsite/uploads/aug16_results.pdf Results from the National Electoral Commission on August 16, 2007] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926041351/http://www.news.sl/drwebsite/uploads/aug16_results.pdf |date=September 26, 2007 }}, news.sl.</ref> Results from about 62% of polling stations on August 17 showed Koroma with 516,442 votes, Berewa with 421,812 votes, and Margai with 169,408 votes.<ref>[http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/08/17/africa/AF-POL-Sierra-Leone-Elections.php "Opposition candidate holds onto lead in Sierra Leone presidential vote count"], Associated Press (''International Herald Tribune''), August 17, 2007.</ref>

On August 18, results from 81% of polling stations showed Koroma with 44% of the vote and Berewa with 38%.<ref>[http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/08/18/africa/AF-POL-Sierra-Leone-Elections.php "Runoff likely for Sierra Leone presidency as opposition candidate leads vote"], Associated Press (''International Herald Tribune''), August 18, 2007.</ref> On August 20, results from 93.1% of polling stations showed Koroma with 754,696 votes, Berewa with 654,756 votes, and Margai with 239,637 votes. In the parliamentary election, the APC had 21 seats, the SLPP had 10, and the PMDC had four. Turnout is placed at about 76%.<ref>[http://www.awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/uploads/aug20_results.pdf Results from the National Electoral Commission on August 20, 2007] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926025527/http://www.awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/uploads/aug20_results.pdf |date=September 26, 2007 }}, news.sl.</ref>

On August 19, Margai stated his support for Koroma in the second round of the election.<ref>[http://awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/publish/article_20056276.shtml "In Sierra Leone, Margai endorses Ernest Koroma at his 62nd Birthday Party"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927225207/http://awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/publish/article_20056276.shtml |date=2007-09-27 }}, ''Awareness Times'', August 19, 2007.</ref> On August 20, the PMDC's Karamoh Kabba explained what he said was the reasoning behind the decision: the PMDC sought to "ensure a more representative government, the survival of the PMDC as a political party and the creation of a third formidable political force for smooth running of democracy in Sierra Leone". According to Kabba, backing the APC would help to bridge the regional political divide marking Sierra Leone's politics (the SLPP and PMDC draw their main support from the south, while the APC draws its main support from the north), while backing the SLPP would deepen it; furthermore, he said that the APC had a natural reason to want the PMDC to survive, while the SLPP had a natural reason to want to destroy it.<ref>[http://awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/publish/article_20056295.shtml "'Why We Went APC' - PMDC's Karamoh Kabba Speaks"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927225225/http://awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/publish/article_20056295.shtml |date=2007-09-27 }}, ''Awareness Times'', August 20, 2007.</ref> Margai's support for the APC provoked controversy in the PMDC, however, with some feeling that his decision was made without properly consulting the party.<ref>Peter Clottey, [http://www.voanews.com/english/Africa/2007-08-21-voa3.cfm "Some Executives of Sierra Leone’s Opposition PMDC Unhappy"]{{dead link|date=April 2025|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}, VOA News, August 21, 2007.</ref><ref>[http://awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/publish/article_20056286.shtml "In Sierra Leone, PMDC Rank & File Condemn Charles Margai"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927225231/http://awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/publish/article_20056286.shtml |date=2007-09-27 }}, ''Awareness Times'', August 20, 2007.</ref>

SLPP spokesman Victor Reider expressed confidence for his party ahead of the second round, to be held in September, and said that the SLPP would warn voters about "the risk of destroying everything that we have been able to put together since 2002 when the war ended".<ref>Kari Barber, [http://voanews.com/english/2007-08-21-voa21.cfm "Sierra Leone Opposition Candidates to Ally Themselves Ahead of Runoff Election"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070821191526/http://voanews.com/english/2007-08-21-voa21.cfm |date=2007-08-21 }}, VOA News, August 21, 2007.</ref>

Full provisional results announced on August 23 showed Koroma with 815,523 votes (44.3%), Berewa with 704,012 votes (38.3%), and Margai with 255,499 votes (13.9%).<ref name=Full>[http://necsierraleone.org/20070823_1500_SummaryNationalProgressive.pdf Full provisional results from the National Electoral Commission] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111001214941/http://necsierraleone.org/20070823_1500_SummaryNationalProgressive.pdf |date=2011-10-01 }}, August 23, 2007.</ref><ref name=Freetown>[http://www.int.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=86&art_id=nw20070823223651419C426830 "Freetown opposition party wins majority"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060615021453/http://www.int.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1 |date=2006-06-15 }}, Reuters (''IOL''), August 24, 2007.</ref> Thorpe confirmed that a second round would be necessary between Koroma and Berewa.<ref name=Freetown/> In the parliamentary election, the APC won 59 seats, the SLPP won 43 seats, and the PMDC won 10 seats.<ref name=Full/><ref name=Freetown/> Turnout was placed at 75.8%.<ref name=Full/>

Results by region reveal how Margai's PMDC party cut into the SLPP's support in the south and east. Especially significant
was the PMDC's winning of 41% of the vote in the SLPP stronghold of Southern Province. In Northern Province, which has the
largest voting population, the APC maintained its support and Koroma got almost 77% of the vote, the largest share by region for any of the candidates. The APC also received strong support in Western Area. Comparing the first-round performances of the SLPP and APC presidential candidates in 2002 and 2007 makes clear the SLPP's loss of ground in the south and east. While in 2002 the SLPP's candidate Kabbah won 92% and 95% in Eastern and Southern provinces, respectively, five years later the SLPP's candidate Berewa won just 66% and 46% there.<ref>Christopher Wyrod, [http://www.journalofdemocracy.org/articles/toc/tocjan08.html "Sierra Leone: A Vote for Better Governance"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080924123311/http://www.journalofdemocracy.org/articles/toc/tocjan08.html |date=2008-09-24 }}, ''Journal of Democracy'', January 2008, Volume 19, Number 1: 77.</ref>

The SLPP's biggest losses were in the Western Area districts around Freetown. While the SLPP won more than half the seats in Western Area parliamentary elections in 2002, voters turned away from the SLPP in droves and the party lost all 21 seats in Western Area to the APC in 2007.<ref>Christopher Wyrod, [http://www.journalofdemocracy.org/articles/toc/tocjan08.html "Sierra Leone: A Vote for Better Governance"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080924123311/http://www.journalofdemocracy.org/articles/toc/tocjan08.html |date=2008-09-24 }}, ''Journal of Democracy'', January 2008, Volume 19, Number 1: 80.</ref>

Margai said on August 24 that the APC and the PMDC would campaign together for the second round. Koroma's campaign called for Berewa to recognize defeat and back out of the second round in order to save the money that would have to be spent on it, but the SLPP refused to do so. Reider predicted victory for Berewa and said that the government should receive credit for being democratic and law-abiding, enabling the APC to win the parliamentary election.<ref>[http://www.int.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=86&art_id=nw20070824192835994C801444 "Sierra Leone opposition unite"], Reuters (''IOL''), August 24, 2007.</ref>

On August 25, the NEC confirmed the provisional results,<ref name=Run>[http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/08/26/africa/AF-POL-Sierra-Leone-Elections.php "Sierra Leone presidential run-off set for Sept. 8"], Associated Press (''International Herald Tribune''), August 25, 2007.</ref> announced that the second round of the presidential election would take place on September 8,<ref name=Set/><ref name=Run/> and said that second round campaigning would take place from August 25 until September 6.<ref name=Run/>

The results were strongly marked by the regional divide between support for the APC, which won overwhelmingly in the north and also by a large margin in the west, and the SLPP, which won in the south and east, although it split its support there with the PMDC. The parties performed poorly outside of their regional support bases;<ref name=Split/><ref>David Farley Keili, [http://www.awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/uploads/keili_elections.htm "Elections 2007 by the Numbers: What the People Said"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927225219/http://www.awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/uploads/keili_elections.htm |date=2007-09-27 }}, ''Awareness Times'', August 27, 2007.</ref> the APC won only two of its 59 parliamentary seats outside of the north and west.<ref name=Split/> Koroma received his best results in [[Bombali District]] (83.9%), [[Tonkolili District]] (82.2%), and [[Port Loko District]] (78.6%), while Berewa received his best results in [[Kailahun District]] (77.1%), [[Kenema District]] (63.0%), and [[Kono District]] (57.1%). Margai received a majority only in [[Bonthe District]] (61.1%).<ref>[http://necsierraleone.org/FinalResults.pdf Final results from the National Electoral Commission] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926025526/http://necsierraleone.org/FinalResults.pdf |date=2007-09-26 }}, August 25, 2007.</ref>

Reider of the SLPP said that, despite "reservations over the conduct of the elections", his party accepted the results. The APC's Victor Foh said that his party accepted the results as well.<ref>[http://www.afriquenligne.fr/news/daily_news/political_parties_accept_sierra_leone_election_results_200708277190/ "Political parties accept Sierra Leone election results"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, Panapress (afriquenligne.fr), August 26, 2007.</ref>

Foh alleged that a paramount chief in an eastern district had brought mercenaries from neighboring [[Guinea]] to intimidate voters by firing near polling stations.<ref>Katrina Manson, [http://www.int.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=86&art_id=nw20070826200448129C604082 "Koroma, Berewa launch campaigns in Freetown"], Reuters (''IOL''), August 26, 2007.</ref>

Two small parties, the [[National Democratic Alliance (Sierra Leone)|National Democratic Alliance]] (NDA) and the [[Peace and Liberation Party]] (PLP), respectively led by [[Amadu Jalloh]], the fifth place presidential candidate, and [[Kandeh Baba Conteh]], the sixth place candidate, announced their support for Berewa in a press conference on August 27.<ref>Aruna Turay, [http://awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/publish/article_20056363.shtml "NDA and PLP join alliance with SLPP for a Berewa Victory in Sierra Leone"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927225200/http://awarenesstimes.com/drwebsite/publish/article_20056363.shtml |date=2007-09-27 }}, ''Awareness Times'', August 27, 2007.</ref> Fourth place candidate [[Andrew Turay]] of the [[Convention People's Party]] (CPP) also announced his support for Berewa in early September.<ref>[http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20056430.shtml "Former Sierra Leone Vice President Declares Support for Current Vice President to become the Next President"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070928043538/http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20056430.shtml |date=2007-09-28 }}, ''Awareness Times'', September 4, 2007.</ref>

After clashes between supporters of the two sides in Freetown and [[Koidu-Sefadu|Koidu Town]], President Kabbah on August 27 warned that he would declare a state of emergency "if the current state of intimidation, molestation and violent acts is not stopped".<ref>[http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/08/28/africa/AF-POL-Sierra-Leone.php "Sierra Leone president threatens to impose state of emergency"], Associated Press (''International Herald Tribune''), August 28, 2007.</ref> A curfew was imposed in Koidu.<ref>[http://apanews.net/apa.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=40494 "Sierra Leone imposes curfew in Kono due to electoral violence"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, August 28, 2007.</ref> In an interview with [[Voice of America]], Reider claimed that Berewa had actually received 20,000 more votes than Koroma and that there were irregularities, particularly in the north and west, regarding which he said the SLPP had requested an investigation from the NEC. He also blamed the violence on the APC.<ref>James Butty, [http://webarchive.loc.gov/all/20070915050814/http://voanews.com/english/Africa/2007-08-29-voa4.cfm?renderforprint=1&textonly=1&&CFID=202519685&CFTOKEN=67756169 "Sierra Leone's Ruling Party Alleges Opposition Irregularities in August Election"], VOA News, August 29, 2007.</ref> Clashes were reported in [[Segbwema]], in the south, on August 30, after SLPP supporters pelted an APC convoy with rocks; the local SLPP headquarters was burned.<ref>{{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20110522100512/http://www.voanews.com/english/2007-08-30-voa82.cfm|title="Sierra Leone's Rival Political Parties Clash Ahead of Elections"}}, VOA News, August 30, 2007.</ref> The violence led Koroma to end his campaigning in the south. Clashes erupted in Freetown on September 1 and reportedly left dozens of people injured; police intervened with [[tear gas]]. On the same day, Koroma and Berewa agreed to hold a peace march on September 3 in hopes of deescalating the violence.<ref>[http://english.aljazeera.net/NR/exeres/ABBF339E-E0DB-430A-9AFB-9E3E2505F889.htm "Election violence hits Sierra Leone"], Al Jazeera, September 1, 2007.</ref><ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/africa/6974523.stm "Election clashes in Sierra Leone"], BBC News, September 1, 2007.</ref> The two candidates also agreed to allow the police to handle their security, instead of entrusting it to private bodyguards.<ref>[http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/09/02/africa/AF-POL-Sierra-Leone.php "Sierra Leone presidential contenders agree to avert violence ahead of poll"], Associated Press (''International Herald Tribune''), September 3, 2007.</ref>

Voter awareness of the concept of a second round and the need for it is said to be low, and it is speculated that voter turnout may be lower in the second round as a result. The NEC has been working to inform voters about the importance of the second round.<ref>Kari Barber, [http://www.voanews.com/english/2007-09-05-voa33.cfm "Election Officials Concerned about Voter Apathy in Sierra Leone Presidential Runoff"]{{dead link|date=April 2025|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}, VOA News, September 5, 2007.</ref>

The peace march agreed to by Berewa and Koroma was eventually held on September 6, but it was boycotted by Koroma.<ref name=Extra>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/africa/6983085.stm "Extra police for S Leone election"], BBC News, September 6, 2007.</ref><ref name=Disappointed>Kari Barber, [http://www.voanews.com/english/2007-09-06-voa60.cfm "Voters Disappointed in Sierra Leone Candidates' Absence From Peace March"]{{dead link|date=April 2025|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}, VOA News, September 6, 2007.</ref> The APC said that the SLPP had not corrected the problems that it said led to the previous violence<ref name=Disappointed/> and alleged that its supporters were being harassed that the [[Kamajors|Kamajor]] militia, active during the [[Sierra Leone Civil War|civil war]], was being rearmed.<ref name=Extra/> Berewa made an appearance, but he did not participate in the actual march.<ref name=Disappointed/>

In the election on September 8, the parties traded accusations of harassment of their polling agents at polling stations.<ref name=Voteend>[http://english.aljazeera.net/NR/exeres/DC9AC4FC-994C-4EE6-94F9-68EAD23EE5D8.htm "Voting ends in Sierra Leone run-off"], Al Jazeera, September 8, 2007.</ref><ref name=Foul>Katrina Manson, [http://www.mg.co.za/articlePage.aspx?articleid=318746&area=/breaking_news/breaking_news__africa/ "Sierra Leone counts votes, both sides cry foul"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120813101109/http://mg.co.za/articlePage.aspx?articleid=318746&area=%2Fbreaking_news%2Fbreaking_news__africa%2F |date=2012-08-13 }}, Reuters (''Mail & Guardian Online''), September 9, 2007.</ref> Berewa alleged that police mistreated SLPP agents<ref name=Voteend/> and the SLPP's Alhaji Jah said there were reports that between 40 and 50 of his party's agents "were harassed, intimidated and taken out of polling stations".<ref name=Foul/> For his part, Koroma alleged that APC representatives were being harassed and kept away from polling stations in Kailahun.<ref name=Voteend/><ref name=Smoothly>[http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/09/09/africa/AF-POL-Sierra-Leone-Elections.php "Presidential runoff in Sierra Leone went smoothly, observers say, urging calm as votes counted"] {{Webarchive|url=https://archive.today/20130103143511/http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/09/09/africa/AF-POL-Sierra-Leone-Elections.php |date=2013-01-03 }}, Associated Press (''International Herald Tribune''), September 9, 2007.</ref> He also said that five APC agents were attacked and kidnapped in Bo and that many people had voted twice in [[Kenema]].<ref name=Foul/> Police imposed a curfew in Kailahun.<ref name=Smoothly/>

European Union observers gave a positive assessment of the day of the election.<ref name=Smoothly/> In a statement on September 10, UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-Moon praised the election as proceeding in a "generally orderly and peaceful atmosphere, in spite of the tensions and violence that marred the campaign period" and urged people to "exercise restraint".<ref>[http://english.people.com.cn/90001/90777/6259553.html "UN chief welcomes "generally" peaceful presidential election in Sierra Leone"], Xinhua (''People's Daily Online''), September 11, 2007.</ref>

As vote counting occurred, both the SLPP and the APC claimed to be ahead and both said that they would reject results from areas where their agents were allegedly not allowed access to polling stations. The SLPP's Reider said that in three districts in the north the party's agents were kept away from the polling stations, and that there were 50 polling stations where SLPP agents were not present; he alleged that SLPP agents were arrested for two hours and that people from the APC took advantage of this to stuff the ballot boxes. APC spokesman Alpha Kanu said that his party's agents had been unable to access polling stations in parts of Kailahun, Kenema, and Pujehun districts in the south.<ref>Susan Njanji, [http://www.sundaytimes.co.za/News/Article.aspx?id=560842 "Sierra Leone awaits poll results"], AFP (''The Times'', South Africa), September 10, 2007.</ref>

The first official results, released by the NEC on September 10, showed Koroma well ahead with 64% of the vote against 36% for Berewa, based on results from slightly more than 20% of polling stations; however, officials said that these results came primarily from the west of the country, where the APC is stronger.<ref name=Secure>Katrina Manson and Christo Johnson, [http://www.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=86&art_id=nw20070910225122227C242668 "Koroma secures early poll lead"], Reuters (''IOL''), September 10, 2007.</ref><ref name=Squabble>[http://www.mg.co.za/articlepage.aspx?area=/breaking_news/breaking_news__africa/&articleid=318907 "Sierra Leone poll rivals squabble over results"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120813101116/http://mg.co.za/articlepage.aspx?area=%2Fbreaking_news%2Fbreaking_news__africa%2F&articleid=318907 |date=2012-08-13 }}, Reuters (''Mail & Guardian Online''), September 11, 2007.</ref> Thorpe emphasized the importance of an "atmosphere of calm" while votes are counted and results are released.<ref name=Secure/> Koroma claimed victory, saying it was "not possible" for him to lose, but Reider of the SLPP accused him of "trying to steal victory" and said that according to the SLPP's figures, Berewa was in the lead. Reider was also strongly critical of a report from the EU's observers that alleged that the number of votes in parts of the south and east exceeded the number of registered voters there.<ref name=Squabble/>

Results released on September 11, based on about 37% of polling stations, showed Koroma with about 60% of the vote and Berewa with about 40%.<ref>[http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/09/11/africa/AF-POL-Sierra-Leone-Elections.php "Opposition candidate leads in Sierra Leone with a third of votes counted"], Associated Press (''International Herald Tribune''), September 11, 2007.</ref> With 50.8% of the vote counted on 12 September,<ref>[http://apanews.net/apa.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=41737 "Sierra Leone: 50% of votes counted, opposition still in lead"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, September 12, 2007.</ref> Koroma had about 58.5%; out of the country's 14 districts, Koroma and Berewa each led in seven of them. On the same day, Kabbah urged people to accept the final results when they are released, regardless of who is declared the winner.<ref>Rod MacJohnson, [http://www.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=68&art_id=nw20070912222615429C663865 "Koroma secures lead as counting continues"], AFP (''IOL''), September 13, 2007.</ref>

Results released on September 13, with 76.1% of the vote counted, showed Koroma leading with 60.2% (859,144 votes) against 39.8% (567,449 votes) for Berewa. The APC's Alpha Kanu said that his party held an "unassailable lead" and was waiting for a concession from Berewa.
<ref>Matthew Green, [https://www.ft.com/content/83742eec-623e-11dc-bdf6-0000779fd2ac "Koroma set for Sierra Leone poll victory"], ''Financial Times'', September 13, 2007.</ref> The SLPP's Reider, however, said that his party was filing a court injunction to prevent the publication of any further results due to what he described as discrepancies in the results. There have been allegations that the NEC has withheld some results from the SLPP's support bases in the south and east, and on September 15 a protest outside NEC offices was broken up by police.<ref>[http://english.aljazeera.net/NR/exeres/08B72C3F-C30E-4E9E-8CC2-CDACFB111DBF.htm "S Leone run-off results questioned"], Al Jazeera, September 16, 2007.</ref>

On September 17, 2007, the NEC said that Koroma had won the election with 54.6% of the vote to Berewa's 45.4%;<ref name=Rod/><ref name=Win/> Koroma had 950,407 votes and Berewa had 789,651.<ref name=Rod/><ref name=SecondFinal>[http://www.necsierraleone.org/FinalResultsSummary20070917.pdf Final second round results] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926025527/http://www.necsierraleone.org/FinalResultsSummary20070917.pdf |date=2007-09-26 }} from the National Electoral Commission, September 17, 2007.</ref> Results from a total of 477 polling stations were invalidated because there were more ballots than there were registered voters:<ref name=SecondFinal/> 426 of these polling stations were in the pro-SLPP Southeast, while 45 were in the [[Northern Province, Sierra Leone|Northern Province]] and six were in the [[Western Area, Sierra Leone|Western Area]]. This decision was taken by Thorpe and two of the four Regional Commissioners; the two remaining Regional Commissioners, representing the south and east, left in protest when Koroma was declared the winner. Berewa promptly conceded defeat.<ref>Bampia James Bundu, [http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20056516.shtml "Solomon Berewa Concedes Defeat to Ernest Bai Koroma in Sierra Leone."] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071014001527/http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20056516.shtml |date=2007-10-14 }}, ''Awareness Times'', September 17, 2007.</ref> According to Kanu, both Kabbah and Berewa called Koroma and congratulated him. Although the SLPP's injunction was scheduled to be heard on the same day, a judge postponed it.<ref name=Rod/> The official results can be challenged within seven days of the official proclamation.<ref name=Rod/><ref name=Win/>

Koroma was sworn in as President at State House in Freetown on 17 September, the same day that the results were announced,<ref name=Healing>Katrina Manson and Christo Johnson, [http://www.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=86&art_id=nw20070917222631534C943067 "Koroma pledges healing in Sierra Leone"], Reuters (''IOL''), September 18, 2007.</ref> at a ceremony attended by Berewa and Kabbah.<ref name=Sworn>[http://english.aljazeera.net/NR/exeres/47DE9DA1-F8B2-4407-8CCD-C7EDF8E53DA5.htm "Sierra Leone's new leader sworn in"], Al Jazeera, September 18, 2007.</ref> Koroma said on this occasion: "Let us begin the process of healing the wounds that suddenly and unnecessarily appeared during the course of this political campaign. Let us endeavour to reconcile ourselves as one nation under God."<ref name=Healing/> Shortly afterward, the SLPP headquarters in Freetown was looted. Police intervened, using tear gas and firing into the air to end the looting; some looters were reported arrested and at least one person was reported killed.<ref name=Sworn/>

The 124 members of parliament were sworn in on September 25. Justice [[Abel Stronge]] was elected as Speaker and the APC's [[Victor Chukuma Johnson]] was elected as Deputy Speaker. [[Edward Turay]] was chosen as Leader of the Majority Party, and [[Momoh Pujeh]] was chosen as Leader of the Minority Party.<ref>Aruna Turay, [http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20056607.shtml "124 Newly Elected Parliamentarians Sworn in Sierra Leone."] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110723014542/http://news.sl/drwebsite/publish/article_20056607.shtml |date=2011-07-23 }}, ''Awareness Times'', September 26, 2007.</ref>

Some in the SLPP have accused Kabbah of contributing to its defeat, alleging that he betrayed the party due to distrust and jealousy that he harbored towards Berewa.<ref>[http://www.afriquenligne.fr/news/daily-news/sierra-leone-opposition-supporters-warn-tejan-kabbah-2007101210503/ "Sierra Leone opposition supporters warn Tejan Kabbah"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071013091158/http://www.afriquenligne.fr/news/daily-news/sierra-leone-opposition-supporters-warn-tejan-kabbah-2007101210503/ |date=2007-10-13 }}, Panapress (afriquenligne.fr), October 12, 2007.</ref>

